=== HINT 1: Set no-new-privs ===
prctl(PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS, 1, 0, 0, 0);
// Required without CAP_SYS_ADMIN before installing a filter

=== HINT 2: Install filter ===
// filter[] and prog already defined in install_filter()
syscall(__NR_seccomp, SECCOMP_SET_MODE_FILTER, 0, &prog);
// Alternative: prctl(PR_SET_SECCOMP, SECCOMP_MODE_FILTER, &prog);

=== HINT 3: Activate sandbox ===
install_filter();  // call from main BEFORE any disallowed syscalls

=== HINT 4: Test kill (optional - process will die) ===
pid_t p = getpid();  // SIGSYS - killed by seccomp filter

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
BPF programs are verified by the kernel before loading - no infinite loops.
SECCOMP_RET_KILL: kill thread immediately (SIGSYS).
SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS: kill entire process (since Linux 4.14).
SECCOMP_RET_ERRNO(e): return error e from syscall (non-fatal).
SECCOMP_RET_TRACE: notify a ptracer (used by strace, sandbox monitors).
SECCOMP_RET_USER_NOTIF: send notification to a listener fd (Linux 5.0) -
  used by container runtimes to intercept syscalls without ptrace overhead.
Chrome renderer, Docker default profile, systemd services all use seccomp-bpf.
